text:
The UAT sign-off covered core replenishment only, for the 25 store pilot cohort, covering ambient and dry goods categories. It explicitly did not extend to fresh categories, which remained in scope for a later phase, and it did not constitute acceptance of the overall programme [1].

The wording was drafted by Ana Duarte on 21 January 2025 and signed by Lena Fischer on 22 January 2025 [1][2]. In the UAT readiness review the same day, Lena Fischer confirmed she was signing that ambient core replenishment works for twenty-five stores, not that the project is finished [3]. At that point eleven business scenarios had been run, nine passed, and two failed on the same rounding root cause (case pack and minimum order quantity); both were scheduled for fix that week and were stated as non-blockers [1][3]. Kwame Boateng later reported both rounding defects fixed and re-tested, with nine of nine scenarios passing [4].

Note on evidence: Lena Fischer's email says "Signed and attached", but the signed attachment itself is not in the supplied records;
